# Narration for "Four ways a neuron starts to fire". Kokoro am_michael. Keys: intro, <regime>_A/B/C, outro.
N = {}
N["intro"] = """Four ways a neuron starts to fire. Every spike is a trip around a loop in a two-dimensional space: membrane voltage, V, left to right, and the potassium gate, n, bottom to top. This is the phase plane. The teal curve is where the voltage stops changing; the orange curve is where the gate stops changing. Where they cross, the cell can sit still — an equilibrium. The injected current, I, is the knob: raise it, and the teal curve lifts. Watch what that does to the crossings. There are exactly four ways a resting state can give way to a cycle. Here they are, one at a time — the phase plane at the current we are at, and on the right, the whole stack of currents in three D."""

N["SNIC_A"] = """Regime one: a saddle-node on an invariant circle. At low current there are three equilibria. A stable node — rest. A saddle — the threshold. And an unstable focus, up at the top. The dashed purple curve is the saddle's stable manifold: the threshold line. Nudge the cell across it, and it makes one full loop — a spike — and comes back to rest along the circle that passes through the saddle and the node. That loop is the invariant circle."""
N["SNIC_B"] = """Now turn up the current. The teal nullcline lifts, and the node and the saddle slide toward each other along the orange curve. At four point six they touch, and annihilate — a saddle-node, sitting right on the circle. The circle is now a limit cycle with no equilibrium on it. Nothing stops the state any more. It just keeps going around."""
N["SNIC_C"] = """Just past the bifurcation the ghost of the vanished node still slows the trajectory, so the first spikes are very slow — the period can be arbitrarily long, and it shortens as the current rises. That is class one excitability: a neuron that can fire at any rate from nearly zero on up. The three-D picture tells the whole story at once: the resting branch in blue, the saddle in purple, and the magenta tube of cycles beginning exactly where they meet."""

N["SN_A"] = """Regime two: a saddle-node off the circle. Same three equilibria — node, saddle, unstable focus — but now the potassium gate is fast, and that changes the geometry. A limit cycle already exists, circling the unstable focus, while the cell is still at rest. Two attractors at the same current: rest, and spiking. Bistability. Nudge the cell across the threshold now, and it does not come back. It is captured by the cycle."""
N["SN_B"] = """Put the cell back at rest, and raise the current. Again the node and the saddle merge at four point six — but this time the cycle is somewhere else, off to the right. When rest disappears, the state falls onto the cycle that was already there."""
N["SN_C"] = """So firing starts at full speed, at a finite frequency — not from zero. And lower the current again: the firing persists below four point six. Hysteresis. In the three-D view the magenta tube reaches far below the plane where the blue and purple branches meet."""

N["subH_A"] = """Regime three: a subcritical Hopf. One equilibrium only — a stable focus. Nudge it a little and the voltage rings back to rest in damped oscillations: a resonator. Around it sits a dashed red ring — an unstable limit cycle, and that ring is the threshold. Outside it, a large stable cycle: spiking. Rest and spiking coexist again, separated by the unstable ring. A big enough nudge jumps the ring, and the cell fires."""
N["subH_B"] = """Back to rest, and raise the current. The unstable ring shrinks onto the focus. At forty-eight point nine it collapses into it, and the focus loses stability — a subcritical Andronov–Hopf bifurcation."""
N["subH_C"] = """The state has nowhere to go but out to the big cycle: full-amplitude spikes from the very first one, with no gradual onset. Bring the current back down and the spiking survives below the Hopf point. Hysteresis again — the hallmark of a subcritical Hopf."""

N["supH_A"] = """Regime four: a supercritical Hopf. One stable focus, damped oscillations — a resonator — and no cycle anywhere."""
N["supH_B"] = """Raise the current. At fourteen point six the focus loses stability, and a tiny limit cycle is born right at the equilibrium. Keep going and it grows — the amplitude rises like the square root of the distance past the bifurcation."""
N["supH_C"] = """So firing starts as small oscillations that swell into spikes, at a frequency that is finite from the very first cycle. In three-D the cycles form a cone, opening upward from the Hopf point."""

N["outro"] = """Four doorways from rest to rhythm. Two saddle-nodes — one on the circle, one off it. Two Hopfs — one subcritical, one supercritical. Integrator or resonator; a firing rate that starts from zero, or from a finite floor; with or without a bistable region where rest and spiking coexist. Stack the phase planes along the current axis and each doorway becomes a shape: a tube that starts where two branches meet, or a cone that opens from a focus. The model is Izhikevich's persistent-sodium-plus-potassium neuron; the code is in the description."""
